WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Hey everybody, let me tell you a story.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.000
So I recently got back into Minecraft after a couple of years away.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.900
I've got a little private friends and family server set up.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.080
I've built some nice things, done a lot of exploring, having a lot of fun.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.858
And then one evening a friend says:

00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:31.540
"Hey. Let's go tame some cats."

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.440
Now I'm of course completely on board with this plan. Pet cats? Sign me up.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.100
So in the current version of Minecraft, this entails finding a village,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.120
because the mechanical spawn trigger for feral cats

00:00:43.128 --> 00:00:46.022
is related to the number of villagers in an area.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.460
So you find a village, and then hang out until you hear a feral cat.

00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:55.080
Then you give the cat some fish until it adopts you, and now you've got a pet cat.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.140
The problem is that just with how our world spawned

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.960
and the way we all chose to disperse around the area before this point,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.420
the closest villages are kind of far away,

00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:09.400
and aren't connected to any of the rivers that we're using as our main transport corridor.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.480
So we make this trip a few times and it's always kind of a hassle.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.760
But hanging out around the village exposes me to a bunch of the other things you can do with villagers

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.740
that the game has added over the years.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.020
See, the last time I played, you could basically only use extremely rare emeralds

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:31.640
to trade for random stuff in a barter system with absolutely no concept of value.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.760
Like you'd have to dig up these emeralds that could only be found

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.740
in one specific type of place in the world (the large hills biome)

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.120
and then some villager would offer you, like

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.500
a pair of leather pants for three emeralds,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.740
and you're just like...

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.920
"Hey. Thanks."

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.480
"There's three cows full of leather right over here, but..."

00:01:51.541 --> 00:01:53.680
"Wow. Great deal."

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:54.720
"Really."

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.840
(It's not really a great deal. That's what we call sarcasm.)

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.720
These days it's a little more coherent and engaging.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.220
Like you go to one villager and you can sell them a bunch of wheat for emeralds

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:09.460
then you can take those emeralds to another villager, and buy a cursed book or something.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.240
Or just hoard emeralds.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.069
Pack them into a block and build a solid emerald bathtub. I'm not your dad.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.720
Anyway, this seems like it could be a lot of fun.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.820
There's still just that one problem: that the closest villages are kind of far away

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.799
and in the wrong direction from everything else we're building,

00:02:25.974 --> 00:02:29.880
and wouldn't it be so much easier for everyone to play with these mechanics

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.220
if we had a village like... right here

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.560
in this giant empty field, located conveniently between a farm, a dark forest,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.260
and a to-scale recreation of Notre Dame carved out of a mountain?

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.220
Yes, it absolutely would be.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.160
So you know what, set to work building a small village with nice little houses

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.240
using the wood and materials that are nearby.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.380
Build a fountain, seed some farms, get it all decorated.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:58.720
Place a bunch of job sites so the villagers have something to do, and...

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:00.345
Now what?

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.580
The game designers have their own vision of how this is supposed to work.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:12.350
They don't really want you to just set up a village of your own convenience, so just building a village isn't enough.

00:03:12.604 --> 00:03:17.524
What they want is for the player to find an existing village and adopt and expand it.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.111
To build up something that's already there.

00:03:20.238 --> 00:03:23.980
And they have constructed mechanics in a way to encourage this approach,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.745
and discourage anything else, by making it really inconvenient.

00:03:27.952 --> 00:03:34.769
You can't just lure villagers with food, or set up a village and expect them to just pop into existence.

00:03:34.992 --> 00:03:40.900
If you want to create a new village without using console commands to just create villagers,

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.849
then you really only have two options:

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.076
The first is to cut down trees until you have an apple.

00:03:46.124 --> 00:03:47.346
Mine eight blocks of gold.

00:03:47.377 --> 00:03:48.821
Smelt the gold into ingots.

00:03:48.846 --> 00:03:51.519
Combine the ingots and apple into a golden apple.

00:03:51.544 --> 00:03:53.210
Build a portal to hell using obsidian.

00:03:53.235 --> 00:03:56.392
Explore hell until you find a nether fortress.

00:03:56.417 --> 00:04:00.932
Search the nether fortress until you encounter several flying enemies called "blaze".

00:04:00.957 --> 00:04:04.280
Kill them until you have at least two blaze rods.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.719
Use one blaze rod to make a brewing stand, and the other to fuel the brewing stand.

00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:11.250
Use a bottle of water plus a fermented spiders eye,

00:04:11.275 --> 00:04:16.815
which is made using a mushroom, sugar, and regular spiders eye, to create a potion of weakness.

00:04:17.053 --> 00:04:20.040
Then add gunpowder to make a splash potion of weakness.

00:04:20.211 --> 00:04:24.068
Hang around your new village waiting for a rare zombie villager to spawn.

00:04:24.162 --> 00:04:25.766
Entomb them in blocks.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.084
Throw the splash potion of weakness on them.

00:04:28.195 --> 00:04:29.465
Feed them the golden apple.

00:04:29.544 --> 00:04:35.036
And then leave them entombed for another 5 minutes real time, so they don't burn up in the sunlight at dawn.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:38.992
And then, finally, the villager will be cured of undeath.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.860
And you'll need to do this twice because one villager does not a village make.

00:04:44.565 --> 00:04:48.809
Alternately, you're going to need to acquire villagers from existing villages.

00:04:49.152 --> 00:04:54.208
...and right here is probably where you pause for a second, squint your eyes, and go:

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:57.810
"Hmm, not so sure about that phrasing there."

00:04:57.835 --> 00:04:59.812
"Not hype on where this is going."

00:04:59.979 --> 00:05:04.680
So at this point I go through the time consuming process of expanding an existing river

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.688
to make it ready for boat traffic.

00:05:06.879 --> 00:05:10.259
Because you see, villagers can't just be lured away.

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:17.458
But if they get into a vehicle, like a boat or a minecart, they can't get out on their own.

00:05:18.129 --> 00:05:22.648
It's just a matter of getting a villager near enough to the water that you can push them into a boat.

00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:28.169
Or maybe you first need to set up an elaborate trap with a minecart, that will first take them down to the river,

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.986
so that you can then push them into a boat,

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:35.727
Sail that boat halfway around the world, and then push them out into their new home.

00:05:35.917 --> 00:05:41.473
And again, of course, you're going to need to this a few times, because a lone villager can't have kids.

00:05:41.616 --> 00:05:47.672
And it may take a village to raise a child, but it also takes a bunch of children to make a village.

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:49.089
And so: yes.

00:05:49.089 --> 00:05:53.577
The best, most straightforward way to create a new village in Minecraft

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:58.679
is to kidnap villagers... from an existing village.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.537
Now this is a good point to say that I don't believe this is intentional.

00:06:02.616 --> 00:06:07.469
That this is more of a weird, emergent outcome of systems interacting.

00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:08.309
I mean,

00:06:08.309 --> 00:06:14.309
I really hope that the people working on Minecraft - both on the Mojang and Microsoft sides of things -

00:06:14.499 --> 00:06:16.299
didn't look at the game and go:

00:06:16.324 --> 00:06:20.308
"Yeah. Human trafficking? Pushing people into a boat in and spiriting them away?"

00:06:20.309 --> 00:06:21.388
"I love it."

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:24.642
That would... that would definitely be a "yikes".

00:06:24.849 --> 00:06:30.476
But it's a particularly interesting case study in the way that game systems create metaphor,

00:06:30.578 --> 00:06:35.392
and that even if you didn't intend for your mechanics to incentivize kidnapping,

00:06:35.417 --> 00:06:43.149
you still sort of created a game where the player is kind of encouraged to do a kidnapping.

00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:45.639
Or two. Or three.

00:06:45.639 --> 00:06:49.988
And it should be noted that this interaction doesn't just mimic kidnapping,

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:54.067
but more specifically the brutal relocation policies of colonialism.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.877
Shove some people in a boat. Translocate them off somewhere else.

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:01.432
Push them out and say: "Okay. This is your home now."

00:07:01.623 --> 00:07:06.090
And the whole uncomfortable metaphor is exacerbated by some of the smaller details.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:11.892
Villagers all have a regional look, so even though they are mechanically identical,

00:07:11.932 --> 00:07:18.815
there's definitely a sense of villagers... taken elsewhere... being out of place.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:23.393
Villagers and the player-character are both humanoid, but distinct from one another.

00:07:23.418 --> 00:07:24.864
They are different groups.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.938
The villagers have large noses, and talk in a nonsense Simlish.

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:35.280
Across multiple vectors, the group with agency - players - are delineated from the group without.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.210
None of these individual details are per se bad,

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:46.461
but they do collectively build a scenario where the player can easily otherize human NPCs.

00:07:47.001 --> 00:07:52.560
This gets pretty dark when players are indirectly incentivized to abduct them,

00:07:52.585 --> 00:07:55.865
or even industrialize them like chattel.

00:07:55.968 --> 00:08:00.809
Such as this contraption, that exploits villagers summoning guardian iron golems

00:08:00.834 --> 00:08:05.183
as an infinite, automated source of iron for making tools.

00:08:05.619 --> 00:08:08.872
As much as I love building and constructing games,

00:08:08.936 --> 00:08:13.048
these kind of issuses, these metaphors, hang over the head of the genre,

00:08:13.073 --> 00:08:18.683
whether they're created from mechanics or from the intrinsic framing of the games' scenario.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:24.532
In the 2019 essay collection "Video Games and the Global South", Jules Skotnes-Brown observes:

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.334
In an era when physical space has been thoroughly explored,

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:32.360
virtual spaces harken back to the romance of the colonial frontier -

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.219
as new regions to discover and conquer.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.430
Such conquest is not just psycho-symbolic,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.357
but also sensitive to the legacies of colonialism and underdevelopment.

00:08:42.531 --> 00:08:46.949
Since most mainstream video games are produced and disseminated in the "developed" world,

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:53.240
they are spaces in which primarily ex-colonial nations can continue to "conquer" the "other",

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.760
even in post-colonial periods.

00:08:55.785 --> 00:09:01.407
In sandbox building games such as Minecraft, the player arrives, like Robinson Crusoe,

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:05.978
into a terra nullius and encourages him to "improve" this land -

00:09:06.169 --> 00:09:10.740
by clearing jungles, draining marshes, building infrastructure and mining minerals.

00:09:10.765 --> 00:09:14.336
Its inhabitants - hostile monsters or local villagers -

00:09:14.448 --> 00:09:20.010
appear simply as obstacles in the path of development, or as resources to exploit."

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:22.170
And he's not wrong.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:28.829
I mean, yeah, a lot of those underlying reasons are why I enjoy construction games, particularly Minecraft.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:33.566
I like exploring. I like building things. I like terraforming.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:38.142
I enjoy bridges and towers and villages and boats and trains.

00:09:38.167 --> 00:09:40.794
And that's not to say that those interests are illegitimate,

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:45.750
but it does speak to deeper cultural values that I have inherited.

00:09:45.925 --> 00:09:55.664
I like terraforming and building big things, in part because I was raised in a culture that values terraforming and building big things.

00:09:55.744 --> 00:10:01.782
A culture that looks at the New York skyline, the Hoover Dam, and the trans-Canada highway,

00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:05.496
and sees first and foremost... "progress".

00:10:05.639 --> 00:10:08.702
A culture that sees these things as "good".

00:10:08.877 --> 00:10:15.030
My interests and values are authentic, but they didn't form in a vacuum.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:19.230
And this is a value set that informs the entire genre.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.227
Minecraft, Factorio, Satisfactory, Stonehearth, Dwarf Fortress,

00:10:23.252 --> 00:10:26.892
Towns, Timber and Stone, No Man's Sky, Gnomoria.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:33.899
Even the relatively constrained Stardew Valley is enamored with the romance of taming the wilderness.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:38.699
It is certainly interesting that in a genre crowded with options,

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:42.980
there are multiple games where the player builds vast labyrinthian factories

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.660
to extract resources on an alien planet,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.600
and not one where the player reclaims a planet from the extractors,

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.660
carefully balancing wetlands and ecosystems.

00:10:53.660 --> 00:11:00.860
I mean: is the food chain of a marsh any less complex than assembling red chips in Factorio?

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:08.130
Now, this is not me saying that all these games form a deliberate block of pro-colonial propaganda.

00:11:08.463 --> 00:11:12.999
In fact, all of them to some degree or another are aware of that legacy,

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:17.220
and try to steer gently away from the issue by fabricating scenarios

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.360
where the behavior is in some way or another justified or permissable.

00:11:21.542 --> 00:11:25.139
All of them have a reason for doing what you're doing.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.885
The protagonist in Factorio, for example, isn't a colonist,

00:11:29.996 --> 00:11:35.631
but is stranded on an alien planet by accident, and given the implicit goal of leaving.

00:11:35.773 --> 00:11:41.281
In No Man's Sky, the protagonist is adrift in a universe where no one belongs.

00:11:41.392 --> 00:11:45.424
A space too vast for any one person to make a meaningful impact.

00:11:45.503 --> 00:11:49.620
A place devoid of villagers to displace.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:53.359
Again, they all have reasons for doing what you're doing.

00:11:53.384 --> 00:11:56.680
But these are just explanations, not excuses.

00:11:56.846 --> 00:12:01.619
In many ways, these works of fiction are indulging in a "clean" version of history.

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:05.280
What if America, but no Native Americans?

00:12:05.505 --> 00:12:11.537
(Also, dear Factorio developers. I love your game, but this wording here... woof.)

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:18.124
This sense of insignificance is a through-line we can see in a lot of these, even, to a degree, Minecraft.

00:12:18.282 --> 00:12:24.187
The relative powerlessness of the individual in the scope of the systems they are interacting with.

00:12:24.378 --> 00:12:32.138
As with No Man's Sky, Minecraft's world is unfathomably vast - about 900 million square kilometres.

00:12:32.281 --> 00:12:38.820
It is impossible for an individual to truly alter the nature of the world.

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.640
But even that narrative isn't new.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:47.143
"Prairie Fires" by Caroline Fraser outlines how tales of rugged individualism were used

00:12:47.168 --> 00:12:53.643
to downplay, excuse and dismiss the institutionalized displacement and massacre of indigenous Americans.

00:12:53.668 --> 00:12:59.936
How, in books like "Little House on the Prairie", authors like Laura Ingalls Wilder took colonization

00:13:00.063 --> 00:13:03.959
and turned it into a thousand tiny, quaint stories.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:08.239
Not the product of policy, culture, and values,

00:13:08.366 --> 00:13:12.201
but the lamentable byproduct of a thousand individuals,

00:13:12.349 --> 00:13:15.924
each too small to truly alter the nature of the world.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:20.340
So, the question then, is how do we navigate this?

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:26.275
It is, perhaps, tempting to fall into an all-or-nothing proposition and just throw the whole lot out.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.969
But that is, in my estimation at least, immature.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:35.782
It is possible to interrogate our collective past, the world that we have inherited,

00:13:35.909 --> 00:13:41.576
and determine that bridges and boats and trains are okay and good.

00:13:41.671 --> 00:13:48.798
That building things and even altering the environment are things that are, or can be, good.

00:13:48.925 --> 00:13:53.623
That it is possible to explore in a way that is better than our own history.

00:13:53.734 --> 00:14:00.539
That all these things are themselves value-neutral, and it is up to us to engage them morally.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:06.140
This is also where there is a great value in ensuring that voices from different backgrounds,

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:09.327
different cultures, are given space to create.

00:14:09.422 --> 00:14:15.437
To maybe argue against permanence, to argue the viewpoints of the colonized.

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.645
Is it possible to tell these kinds of stories?

00:14:18.756 --> 00:14:27.599
Stories of exploring the unknown, without creating a fantasized, idealized version of our own colonialist past?

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:33.479
I... I don't know. I don't have an answer for that.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.100
Maybe?

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.960
And at the very least, maybe we won't end up with games

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.457
where you push villagers into boats to haul them away.

